1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,000
[1/5 THE CHALLENGE] Hello everyone, and respected judges! Today, modern applications are built using hundreds of open-source packages across npm, Python, Go, and GitHub workflows.

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
[1/5 THE CHALLENGE] While open-source libraries help us build products faster, managing their security has become extremely complex.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,500
[1/5 THE CHALLENGE] In large codebases, applications often contain multiple levels of nested dependencies.

4
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:36,000
[1/5 THE CHALLENGE] The real challenge for engineering teams is knowing: Which vulnerabilities are actually reachable in production code, and which ones are never called?

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:48,000
[1/5 THE CHALLENGE] Without this reachability context, developers can spend weeks investigating alerts or risk breaking live backend APIs during package upgrades.

6
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,380
[1/5 THE CHALLENGE] This is why modern software security requires a deeper, code-aware approach.

7
00:00:54,380 --> 00:01:02,000
[2/5 PROPOSED SOLUTION] To address this challenge, we created SupplyGraph.

8
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:12,000
[2/5 PROPOSED SOLUTION] SupplyGraph is an Application Security Posture Management platform designed to give developers complete visibility into their software supply chain.

9
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:22,000
[2/5 PROPOSED SOLUTION] Instead of treating dependencies as flat isolated lists, SupplyGraph analyzes project call paths and builds an interactive visual attack graph.

10
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,000
[2/5 PROPOSED SOLUTION] It connects your code from user-facing entry points all the way down to deeply nested libraries.

11
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
[2/5 PROPOSED SOLUTION] It clearly verifies whether an execution path actually reaches a vulnerable component.

12
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:48,580
[2/5 PROPOSED SOLUTION] When an issue is identified, SupplyGraph provides safe, line-by-line code patches that preserve application functionality, while testing untrusted packages inside an isolated sandbox.

13
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:54,000
[3/5 CORE FEATURES] Let us look at four core capabilities of SupplyGraph:

14
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:07,000
[3/5 CORE FEATURES] First is the Attack Graph. Powered by graph traversal algorithms, it visually maps the relationship between code and libraries, showing exact reachability paths.

15
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:19,000
[3/5 CORE FEATURES] Second is the Blast Radius Score. It calculates a clear risk metric from 0 to 100, helping teams understand potential operational impact.

16
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:33,500
[3/5 CORE FEATURES] Third is the Autonomous Executive AI. It summarizes technical vulnerability data into actionable briefings and prioritized containment steps.

17
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:49,540
[3/5 CORE FEATURES] And fourth is the CycloneDX SBOM. It generates standard Software Bill of Materials documents with deterministic SHA-256 cryptographic verification for compliance.

18
00:02:49,540 --> 00:02:57,000
[4/5 DEMO SCENARIOS] To demonstrate these capabilities, SupplyGraph includes five preloaded real-world scenarios:

19
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:08,000
[4/5 DEMO SCENARIOS] Scenario A illustrates a transitive Log4j call path, mapping how an incoming request travels through service layers to a vulnerable logging library.

20
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:18,000
[4/5 DEMO SCENARIOS] Scenario B examines Prototype Pollution, showing how improper object merging can be safely identified and corrected.

21
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:28,000
[4/5 DEMO SCENARIOS] Scenario C analyzes insecure Python pickle deserialization, demonstrating AST-level detection before code execution.

22
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:39,000
[4/5 DEMO SCENARIOS] Scenario D audits GitHub Actions workflows, ensuring runners and third-party actions use immutable commit hashes.

23
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:52,180
[4/5 DEMO SCENARIOS] And Scenario E tests lifecycle scripts, demonstrating how package installation hooks are monitored and controlled.

24
00:03:52,180 --> 00:04:02,000
[5/5 REAL PROJECT WALKTHROUGH] Now let us examine a real-world open-source repository: listmonk by Kailash Nadh.

25
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:13,500
[5/5 REAL PROJECT WALKTHROUGH] When scanning a multi-module repository like this, SupplyGraph indexes all 35 submodules and manifests, providing a unified inventory across frontend, backend, and workflows.

26
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:27,000
[5/5 REAL PROJECT WALKTHROUGH] It identifies 21 findings across direct and transitive dependencies, computing a contextual Blast Radius score of 60 out of 100.

27
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:41,000
[5/5 REAL PROJECT WALKTHROUGH] With the 'Fix with AI' feature, SupplyGraph generates evidence-based, non-breaking remediation prompts specifically structured for AI coding agents like Antigravity, Cursor, and Copilot.

28
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:55,000
[5/5 REAL PROJECT WALKTHROUGH] In the AI Code Reviewer, developers inspect surgical unified git diffs that correct specific vulnerable lines rather than forcing broad version jumps.

29
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:09,240
[5/5 REAL PROJECT WALKTHROUGH] And in our Detonation Sandbox, lifecycle scripts run within an isolated container where unauthorized network connections and file modifications are safely contained.
